<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ternopil</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ternopil is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> on the Seret River, in the historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a>. It was founded in 1540 as a fortress by the Polish commander Jan Amor Tarnowski and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in the partitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. In November and December 1918, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> was lost to Polish forces, it was briefly the seat of the government of the West Ukrainian People's Republic. It is now the centre of Ternopil Oblast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ternopil.jpg" alt="Ternopil" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Антон Марчевський (Buran) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UA-TE_Ternopil_Buran_18-06-16.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ternopil, known until 1944 mostly as Tarnopol, stands on the banks of the Seret River in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and belongs to the historical regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a>. Its Polish name, Tarnopol, means Tarnowski's city. The city began as a military stronghold and castle protecting the eastern borders of the Polish kingdom from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> raids. On 15 April 1540 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, gave Jan Amor Tarnowski permission to establish it near Sopilche.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1570 the city passed to the Ostrogski family and in 1623 to the Zamoyski family. Turkish forces under Ibrahim Shishman Pasha almost completely destroyed it in 1675, during the Polish–Ottoman War, and Aleksander Koniecpolski rebuilt it. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772 it came under Austrian rule; it was under Russian rule from 1809 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1815. A railway linked it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lemberg</a> in 1870, and a Ukrainian gymnasium opened in 1898. In the late 19th and early 20th centuries it was the only urban locality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> where ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> outnumbered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>.</p><p>It was proclaimed part of the West Ukrainian People's Republic on 11 November 1918 and became its temporary capital after Polish forces captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a>. Polish forces took it on 15 July 1919, and in the interwar period it was the capital of the Tarnopol Voivodeship. Soviet troops entered on 17 September 1939, and the city was incorporated into the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic. After German occupation began on 2 July 1941, 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were killed in the city by July 1943 and another 6,000 were sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belzec-extermination-camp">Belzec</a> extermination camp. The Soviet offensive of 1944 almost completely destroyed it, and it was rebuilt in a Soviet style.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ternopil is the administrative centre of Ternopil Oblast and has been part of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> since 1991. It is an important railway hub, and its light, food, electronics and construction industries grew under Soviet rule. Its universities include the West Ukrainian National University and the Ternopil Ivan Pul'uj National Technical University.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ternopil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Polish residents of Lviv resisted, and the Polish-Ukrainian War began; the Ukrainians lost Lviv in November and moved the capital to <b>Ternopil</b> and then Stanislaviv.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-ukrainian-peoples-republic">West Ukrainian People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
